Magpie model gateway

Install:
  Install missing Magpie through the official terminal release installer.
  Configure and start its gateway as a systemd user service with the Web interface disabled; enable startup at boot and continued operation after logout.

Upgrade:
  Update the managed Magpie executable to the current terminal release and refresh the gateway service with the invoking shell's environment; skip when the executable is absent.

Status:
  Report the managed executable version, daemon state, gateway availability and user lingering through bounded local queries.

Requirements:
  Ubuntu on amd64 or arm64, APT and a regular user with sudo access.
  A running systemd instance and an accessible systemd user manager; WSL must have systemd enabled.
  Installation and upgrades require access to usemagpie.ai and its release download sources.

Configuration:
  Executable: ~/.local/bin/magpie
  User service: ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/systemd/user/magpie.service
  The official installer verifies release checksums; the module manages the executable and gateway service.
  Both install and upgrade run magpie daemon install --no-web, refreshing the service even when the executable is already installed.
  The service inherits supported settings, including PATH and proxy variables, from the invoking shell.
  User lingering keeps the user's enabled services available at boot and after logout.
  The gateway uses Magpie's configuration, with 127.0.0.1:3425 as the upstream default.
  Run magpie tui or magpie web to manage providers and models; the Web interface runs only when requested.
  Providers, credentials and agent model selection are configured through Magpie by the user.
